To extract text from a document using JavaScript, you can use various libraries depending on the type of document (e.g., PDF, Word, HTML). Below is an example of how to extract text from an HTML document using plain JavaScript.

If you're working with other types of documents like PDFs or Word files, you might need additional libraries such as `pdf-lib` for PDFs or `mammoth.js` for Word documents.

Here's a simple example for extracting text from an HTML document:

```javascript
function extractTextFromHTML(html) {
    // Create a temporary div element to hold the HTML content
    const tempDiv = document.createElement('div');

    // Set the innerHTML of the temporary div to the provided HTML string
    tempDiv.innerHTML = html;

    // Extract all text nodes and concatenate them into one string
    let textContent = '';
    const walker = document.createTreeWalker(tempDiv, NodeFilter.SHOW_TEXT);
    while (walker.nextNode()) {
        textContent += walker.currentNode.nodeValue.trim() + ' ';
    }

    return textContent.trim();
}

// Example usage:
const htmlContent = `
<html>
<head><title>Sample Document</title></head>
<body>
<h1>Welcome to My Website</h1>
<p>This is a sample paragraph.</p>
<ul>
<li>Item 1</li>
<li>Item 2</li>
</ul>
</body>
</html>
`;

console.log(extractTextFromHTML(htmlContent));
```

This function creates a temporary `div` element, sets its `innerHTML` to the provided HTML string, and then uses a tree walker to traverse through all text nodes within this `div`. It concatenates these text nodes into a single string, which represents the extracted text from the HTML document.

