Yes, in Node.js, you can implement a pull-based stream by using the `stream` module and creating your own custom stream class that extends the `Readable` or `Transform` classes.

A pull-based stream allows data to be pulled from it when needed, rather than pushing data onto it as soon as it's available (which is how push-based streams work). This gives you more control over when and how much data is processed.

Here’s an example of how you might create a simple pull-based stream:

```javascript
const { Readable } = require('stream');

class PullBasedStream extends Readable {
  constructor(options) {
    super(options);
    this.data = ['data1', 'data2', 'data3']; // Example data array
    this.index = 0;
  }

  _read() {
    if (this.index < this.data.length) {
      const chunk = this.data[this.index++];
      this.push(chunk); // Push the next chunk of data
    } else {
      this.push(null); // Signal end of stream
    }
  }
}

// Usage
const pullStream = new PullBasedStream();

pullStream.on('data', (chunk) => {
  console.log(`Received: ${chunk}`);
});

pullStream.on('end', () => {
  console.log('End of stream');
});
```

In this example:
- The `_read()` method is called internally by the `Readable` class whenever there is demand for more data.
- Inside `_read()`, we check if there is any remaining data in our internal buffer (`this.data`). If so, we push the next chunk of data into the stream using `this.push(chunk)`.
- When all data has been pushed, we call `this.push(null)` to signal the end of the stream.

This approach gives you fine-grained control over when data is emitted from the stream, making it suitable for scenarios where you need to process large amounts of data incrementally without loading everything into memory at once.

