To prevent XPath injection, you should avoid directly concatenating user input into your XPath expressions. Instead, use parameterized queries or prepared statements if possible. However, since Java's standard libraries do not support parameterized XPath queries natively, we can sanitize the inputs by escaping special characters.

Here’s how you can modify your code to escape special characters in the `user` and `pass` variables:

```java
import org.apache.commons.text.StringEscapeUtils;

public class SafeXPathExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Example user input (in real scenarios, this would come from an external source)
        String user = "<script>alert('XSS');</script>john";
        String pass = "password";

        // Escape special characters in the user and password strings
        String escapedUser = StringEscapeUtils.escapeXml10(user);
        String escapedPass = StringEscapeUtils.escapeXml10(pass);

        // Construct the safe XPath expression
        String expression = "/users/user[@name='" + escapedUser + "' and @pass='" + escapedPass + "']";

        System.out.println("Safe XPath Expression: " + expression);
    }
}
```

In this example, I used Apache Commons Text library for XML escaping. If you don't have it available, you could implement a simple method to escape special characters manually:

```java
public class SafeXPathExample {
    private static final String[] SPECIAL_CHARS = {"&", "<", ">", "\"", "'", "\\", "/", "?"};

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Example user input (in real scenarios, this would come from an external source)
        String user = "<script>alert('XSS');</script>john";
        String pass = "password";

        // Escape special characters in the user and password strings
        String escapedUser = escapeSpecialChars(user);
        String escapedPass = escapeSpecialChars(pass);

        // Construct the safe XPath expression
        String expression = "/users/user[@name='" + escapedUser + "' and @pass='" + escapedPass + "']";

        System.out.println("Safe XPath Expression: " + expression);
    }

    private static String escapeSpecialChars(String input) {
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
        for (char c : input.toCharArray()) {
            boolean isSpecialChar = false;
            for (String specialChar : SPECIAL_CHARS) {
                if (c == specialChar.charAt(0)) {
                    isSpecialChar = true;
                    break;
                }
            }
            if (isSpecialChar) {
                switch (c) {
                    case '&':
                        sb.append("&amp;");
                        break;
                    case '<':
                        sb.append("&lt;");
                        break;
                    case '>':
                        sb.append("&gt;");
                        break;
                    case '"':
                        sb.append("&quot;");
                        break;
                    case '\'':
                        sb.append("&apos;");
                        break;
                    case '\\':
                        sb.append("\\\\");
                        break;
                    case '/':
                        sb.append("/\\");
                        break;
                    case '?':
                        sb.append("?\\");
                        break;
                    default:
                        throw new IllegalArgumentException("Unsupported character: " + c);
                }
            } else {
                sb.append(c);
            }
        }
        return sb.toString();
    }
}
```

This approach ensures that any potentially harmful characters in the user input are properly escaped before being included in the XPath expression, thus mitigating the risk of XPath injection attacks.

