Here's an example of a recursive JavaScript function that retries a request up to three times before resolving the promise:

```javascript
function retryRequest(requestFunction, maxRetries = 3) {
    return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
        const attempt = (retryCount) => {
            requestFunction()
                .then(resolve)
                .catch((error) => {
                    if (retryCount < maxRetries) {
                        console.log(`Attempt ${retryCount + 1} failed. Retrying...`);
                        setTimeout(() => attempt(retryCount + 1), 1000); // Retry after 1 second delay
                    } else {
                        console.error('Max retries reached. Request failed.');
                        reject(error);
                    }
                });
        };

        attempt(0);
    });
}

// Example usage:
const makeRequest = () => {
    return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
        // Simulate a network request with a random success or failure
        const isSuccess = Math.random() > 0.5;
        if (isSuccess) {
            resolve('Request succeeded!');
        } else {
            reject(new Error('Request failed!'));
        }
    });
};

retryRequest(makeRequest).then(result => {
    console.log(result);
}).catch(error => {
    console.error(error.message);
});
```

In this code snippet, `retryRequest` is a higher-order function that takes another function (`requestFunction`) as its argument and returns a new promise. The inner `attempt` function handles the actual retry logic recursively until either the request succeeds or the maximum number of retries is reached.

The `makeRequest` function simulates a network request by randomly deciding whether to succeed or fail. You can replace this with your own asynchronous operation.

When calling `retryRequest`, you pass in the function representing the request you want to retry along with any additional options like `maxRetries`. In case of success, the resolved value will be passed to the `.then()` handler; otherwise, the error message from the last rejected attempt will be logged via the `.catch()` handler.
